00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.749 align:start position:0%
in indonesia's north kalimantan region  palm oil farmers prepare for business as  usual as an export ban comes to an end  the export ban caused a huge loss for us  the price fell so low it really hits us  farmers  indonesia is the world's largest palm  oil producer palm oil exports had been  banned for three weeks as a way to help  ease domestic problems with the price  and availability of cooking oil while  there's been rising international demand  the government will also reintroduce a  domestic market obligation for palm oil  producers forcing them to sell their  products locally at a set price  from what we've seen in the field the  price of bulk oil is slowly declining as  the cooking oil supply is becoming more  abundant  experts criticized the export ban as  misguided  it also set a precedence a very negative  precedence to other producer countries  if they  they have some difficulties in the  domestic market they just cut off the  export or ban the export then  it would make the global food security  is in disarray  farmers held regular protests against  the export ban and say the government  needs to find a better solution in the  future  so we see that there is a mistake made  in the chain of distribution it seems  like this country didn't realize that  cooking oil is a primary need meanwhile  they give it to all private sector  like millions around the world  indonesians are noticing they're paying  more for the essentials shoppers and  business owners say the volatility of  food prices is making life harder for  them cooking oil prices have fallen  since the export ban was introduced but  they're still higher than the prices  people are used to  the government had initially planned to  keep the export ban in place until  prices reached 96 cents per litre that  target has not yet been met  we had to raise our prices because the  price of meat is going up and the price  of cooking oil is still up it's very  difficult for us  the government says it will continue to  monitor prices to make sure indonesians  can afford the essentials but many here  say they are already struggling  jessica washington al jazeera jakarta
 

